drm: sunxi_drm_edp: pin-assignment-aware, bandwidth-aware DP link policy

DP 1.4 picks the link configuration from the intersection of four constraints:
source capability, sink capability, the physical channel, and the bandwidth
the mode needs. edp_update_capacity() applied the first two, and only when
DPCD had been read successfully:

	if (drm_edp->dpcd_parsed && !edp_debug->lane_debug_en) {
		lane_para->lane_cnt = min(src_cap->max_lane, sink_cap->max_lane);
		lane_para->bit_rate = min(src_cap->max_rate, sink_cap->max_rate);
	}

Two things are wrong with that.

The physical channel is missing. On Type-C the DP alt mode pin assignment
decides how many lanes DP actually gets: pin C/E give it all four, pin D/F
give it two and leave the other two to USB3. The vendor left a FIXME here
asking exactly this question and never answered it.

And the whole policy is skipped when DPCD is unavailable, which is precisely
when a sane default matters most. On a failed plug-time DPCD read lane_para
kept the devicetree values - edp_lane_cnt = <4> on this board - and the driver
trained four lanes into a pin-D link with two lanes wired. The two lanes that
go nowhere never reach clock recovery, so the swing walk runs to its ceiling
and training dies with "swing voltage reach max level, training1(clock
recovery training) fail". It also made src_max_lane_debug look inert, since
that attribute is read inside the branch that was being skipped.

Restructure the function as a real link policy maker:

  - Ask the PHY for the lane budget. The combo PHY already tracks the
    negotiated pin assignment and rejects an over-wide config through its
    ->validate op, so query that rather than re-deriving it. A PHY with no
    ->validate accepts everything, leaving eDP panels on four lanes as before.
  - Apply the budget unconditionally, DPCD or not. Without DPCD, narrow the
    existing configuration but never widen it on a guess.
  - Choose the slowest standard rate that still carries the mode instead of
    taking the rate from the devicetree. DP prefers more lanes at a lower
    symbol rate, which also has more margin on a marginal channel.

The bandwidth constraint needs the timings, which are only known after
atomic_mode_set, so drm_dp_output_enable() re-runs the policy once the mode
has been chosen. The plug-time call still applies the capability and pin
constraints as early as possible.

Signed-off-by: Paul Goldsmith <goldfish.paul@gmail.com>
---
--- a/bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_drm_edp.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_drm_edp.c
@@ -889,10 +889,71 @@
 	//TODO
 }
 
-/* FIXME: we need to think about that: typec-dp out's 2LNAE+USB case,
- * how DisplayPort's report its DPCD capbility? if it report
- * 4lane max support, how to compatible with 2LANE+USB case?
- * when should we update the lane para and phy para? */
+/*
+ * The standard DP link rates, ascending. A source should train the slowest
+ * rate that still carries the mode: a lower symbol rate has more margin on a
+ * marginal channel, and DP prefers "more lanes, slower" over "fewer, faster".
+ */
+static const u64 edp_link_rates[] = {
+	BIT_RATE_1G62, BIT_RATE_2G7, BIT_RATE_5G4, BIT_RATE_8G1,
+};
+
+/*
+ * What a link carries, in the same units as pixel_clk * bpp.
+ * DP 8b/10b encoding puts 8 payload bits on the wire for every 10 symbol
+ * bits, so one lane at bit_rate carries bit_rate/10 bytes per second.
+ */
+static u64 edp_link_capacity(u64 bit_rate, u32 lane_cnt)
+{
+	return (bit_rate / 10) * lane_cnt * 8;
+}
+
+/*
+ * How many lanes the physical channel actually has.
+ *
+ * On Type-C that is the DP alt mode pin assignment: pin C/E give DP all four
+ * high-speed lanes, pin D/F give it two and leave the other two to USB3. The
+ * combo PHY already tracks the negotiated assignment and rejects an over-wide
+ * configuration through its ->validate op, so ask it instead of duplicating
+ * the knowledge here. A PHY without ->validate accepts everything, which
+ * leaves non-Type-C (eDP panel) setups at the full four lanes as before.
+ */
+static u32 edp_phy_lane_budget(struct sunxi_drm_edp *drm_edp)
+{
+	u32 lane_cnt;
+
+	for (lane_cnt = 4; lane_cnt > 1; lane_cnt >>= 1)
+		if (edp_phy_validate_lane_cnt(drm_edp, lane_cnt) == 0)
+			break;
+
+	return lane_cnt;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The DP link policy maker: choose the (lane_cnt, bit_rate) to train at.
+ *
+ * DP 1.4 picks that from the intersection of four constraints. The vendor
+ * code applied only the first two, and only when DPCD had been read:
+ *
+ *   1. source capability                                  - was applied
+ *   2. sink capability, from DPCD 0x001/0x002             - was applied
+ *   3. the physical channel: on Type-C the pin assignment - was NOT applied
+ *   4. the bandwidth the mode needs                       - was NOT applied
+ *
+ * Constraint 3, and the fact that the whole thing sat behind "did DPCD read
+ * work", is what made this board fail. A plug whose first DPCD read did not
+ * land left lane_para holding the devicetree defaults - edp_lane_cnt = <4> -
+ * and the driver then trained four lanes into a Type-C pin-D link that only
+ * has two of them wired to DP. That cannot recover: the two lanes that go
+ * nowhere never reach clock recovery, so the training loop walks the drive
+ * swing up to its maximum and gives up ("swing voltage reach max level,
+ * training1(clock recovery training) fail"). It also explains why the
+ * src_max_lane_debug attribute appeared to do nothing - it is read here, and
+ * this function was returning early.
+ *
+ * So the pin budget is now applied unconditionally, DPCD or no DPCD, and the
+ * rate is chosen to fit the mode rather than taken from the devicetree.
+ */
 static void edp_update_capacity(struct sunxi_drm_edp *drm_edp)
 {
 	struct edp_tx_core *edp_core = &drm_edp->edp_core;
@@ -900,22 +961,68 @@
 	struct edp_tx_cap *src_cap = &drm_edp->source_cap;
 	struct edp_lane_para *lane_para = &edp_core->lane_para;
 	struct edp_debug *edp_debug = &drm_edp->edp_debug;
+	u32 lane_cnt, lane_budget;
+	u64 bit_rate, need;
+	int i;
+
+	/* the lane_cfg_debug attributes drive lane_para directly, keep off */
+	if (edp_debug->lane_debug_en)
+		return;
+
+	lane_budget = edp_phy_lane_budget(drm_edp);
+
+	/* constraint 1: the source, or whatever src_max_*_debug forces */
+	lane_cnt = edp_debug->src_max_lane ? edp_debug->src_max_lane : src_cap->max_lane;
+	bit_rate = edp_debug->src_max_rate ? edp_debug->src_max_rate : src_cap->max_rate;
+
+	if (drm_edp->dpcd_parsed) {
+		/* constraint 2: what the sink says it can take */
+		lane_cnt = min(lane_cnt, sink_cap->max_lane);
+		bit_rate = min(bit_rate, sink_cap->max_rate);
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * No DPCD yet, so there is nothing better than what is already
+		 * configured - from the devicetree on the first pass. Narrow it
+		 * if the source is smaller, but never widen it on a guess. The
+		 * result can stay narrow across a re-plug into a wider pin
+		 * assignment; the first successful DPCD parse recomputes it
+		 * from source and sink and undoes that.
+		 */
+		lane_cnt = min(lane_cnt, lane_para->lane_cnt);
+		bit_rate = min(bit_rate, lane_para->bit_rate);
+	}
+
+	/* constraint 3: never wider than the pins DP was actually given */
+	lane_cnt = min(lane_cnt, lane_budget);
+	if (!lane_cnt)
+		lane_cnt = 1;
 
 	/*
-	 * edp_debug's src_max_lane/src_max_rate can use for force specific
-	 * lane/rate output for debug purpose
+	 * Constraint 4: with a mode chosen, drop to the slowest standard rate
+	 * that still carries it. This also runs at plug time, before there is
+	 * a mode - pixel_clk is 0 there and the fastest common rate stands.
 	 */
-	if (drm_edp->dpcd_parsed && !edp_debug->lane_debug_en) {
-		if (edp_debug->src_max_lane)
-			lane_para->lane_cnt = min(edp_debug->src_max_lane, sink_cap->max_lane);
-		else
-			lane_para->lane_cnt = min(src_cap->max_lane, sink_cap->max_lane);
-
-		if (edp_debug->src_max_rate)
-			lane_para->bit_rate = min(edp_debug->src_max_rate, sink_cap->max_rate);
-		else
-			lane_para->bit_rate = min(src_cap->max_rate, sink_cap->max_rate);
+	need = (u64)edp_core->timings.pixel_clk * lane_para->bpp;
+	if (need) {
+		for (i = 0; i < (int)ARRAY_SIZE(edp_link_rates); i++) {
+			if (edp_link_rates[i] > bit_rate)
+				break;
+			if (edp_link_capacity(edp_link_rates[i], lane_cnt) >= need) {
+				bit_rate = edp_link_rates[i];
+				break;
+			}
+		}
 	}
+
+	if ((lane_cnt != lane_para->lane_cnt) || (bit_rate != lane_para->bit_rate))
+		EDP_DRV_DBG("link policy: %u lane(s) @ %lluMbps (pin budget:%u src:%u/%lluMbps sink:%u/%lluMbps dpcd:%d)\n",
+			    lane_cnt, bit_rate / 1000000, lane_budget,
+			    src_cap->max_lane, src_cap->max_rate / 1000000,
+			    sink_cap->max_lane, sink_cap->max_rate / 1000000,
+			    drm_edp->dpcd_parsed);
+
+	lane_para->lane_cnt = lane_cnt;
+	lane_para->bit_rate = bit_rate;
 }
 
 static void sink_cap_reset(struct sunxi_drm_edp *drm_edp)
@@ -3949,6 +4056,14 @@
 		EDP_ERR("timing is not set by edid or dts, please check!\n");
 		return RET_FAIL;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Re-run the link policy now that a mode has been chosen. The plug-time
+	 * call could only apply the capability constraints; the bandwidth one
+	 * needs the timings, which atomic_mode_set has just filled in.
+	 */
+	edp_update_capacity(drm_edp);
+
 	clk_set_rate(drm_edp->clk, edp_core->timings.pixel_clk / edp_core->pixel_mode);
 	ret = edp_hw_enable(edp_hw, edp_core);
 	if (ret) {
